.TH KLVTOOL 1 "1970-01-01" "klvtool dev" "User Commands"
.SH "NAME"
klvtool \- CLI for inspecting MPEG\-TS streams, extracting KLV payloads, packetizing raw checkpoints, and decoding MISB metadata.
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
klvtool <command> [flags]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
klvtool is a Go CLI for working with MPEG\-TS video assets and the KLV metadata streams carried inside them. The primary audience is intelligence analysts triaging full\-motion video from sensor platforms (UAS, manned ISR) and engineers integrating KLV pipelines.
.PP
The canonical entry point for triaging an unknown file is `klvtool diagnose \-\-input <file.ts>` — it runs the full health check, transport inspection, video bitstream analysis, and KLV decode in one pass and points to a remediation when something fails.
.SH "COMMANDS"
.TP
\fBklvtool\-version\fR(1)
Print the klvtool version, optionally checking GitHub for a newer release.
.TP
\fBklvtool\-update\fR(1)
Update klvtool to the latest GitHub release.
.TP
\fBklvtool\-doctor\fR(1)
Check backend availability, detected versions, and install guidance.
.TP
\fBklvtool\-extract\fR(1)
Extract raw KLV/data payloads from an MPEG\-TS file and write a manifest.
.TP
\fBklvtool\-inspect\fR(1)
Inspect an MPEG\-TS stream inventory, packet counts, and continuity diagnostics.
.TP
\fBklvtool\-decode\fR(1)
Decode MISB ST 0601 KLV records from an MPEG\-TS file.
.TP
\fBklvtool\-packetize\fR(1)
Replay raw extraction checkpoints into KLV packet checkpoints with framing diagnostics.
.TP
\fBklvtool\-diagnose\fR(1)
Run the full diagnostic pipeline: health check, transport inspect, video analysis, and KLV decode.
.TP
\fBklvtool\-completion\fR(1)
Generate shell completion scripts (bash, zsh, fish).
.SH "WORKFLOWS"
.SS Triage an unknown mission file
You just received a TS file and need to know whether it is usable, whether the metadata is intact, and what to do if something looks wrong.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ klvtool diagnose --input mission.ts
.fi
.RE
runs health check, transport inspect, video analysis, and KLV decode; produces a single consolidated report with remediation guidance for any failed stage
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ klvtool decode --input mission.ts --pid <PID> --format text
.fi
.RE
if diagnose surfaces decode errors on a specific PID, this shows the per\-packet diagnostics in pretty text view
.SS Geo\-locate a frame at a known timestamp
You need sensor latitude/longitude (or frame\-center geo) for a specific moment in the stream and intend to hand the result off to GIS or a downstream pipeline.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ klvtool inspect --input mission.ts
.fi
.RE
find the metadata PID — the row labeled "Likely metadata" in pretty view
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ klvtool decode --input mission.ts --pid <PID> --format ndjson --out frames.ndjson
.fi
.RE
decode only that PID into stable NDJSON; one record per KLV packet, with tag 2 carrying the wall\-clock timestamp
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ jq 'select(.items[] | .tag == 2 and .value >= "2024-01-15T14:23:45.000000Z")' frames.ndjson
.fi
.RE
filter to your timestamp; the surrounding record carries tags 13/14 (sensor lat/lon) and 23/24 (frame\-center lat/lon)
.SS Forensic recovery from corrupt transport
Diagnose reports KLV decode errors or framing problems and re\-pulling the file is not an option.
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ klvtool extract --input mission.ts --out ./mission-extract
.fi
.RE
capture raw KLV payloads and a manifest with SHA\-256 hashes and packet\-level transport metadata for chain of custody
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ klvtool packetize --input ./mission-extract --out ./mission-packets --mode best-effort
.fi
.RE
scan past malformed packets; per\-record diagnostics include byte offsets where the wire format diverged
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ klvtool decode --input mission.ts --pid <PID> --raw --strict
.fi
.RE
with \-\-raw, NDJSON includes base64 of the offending bytes — useful when escalating to platform / sensor engineering
.SH "GLOSSARY"
.TP
\fBRaw checkpoint\fR
the per\-record artifact written by `klvtool extract`: the full PES payload bytes plus transport metadata. Input format for `klvtool packetize`.
.TP
\fBPacket checkpoint\fR
the parsed\-KLV\-packet artifact written by `klvtool packetize`: per\-packet key/length/value byte ranges plus parser diagnostics. One JSON file per source raw checkpoint.
.TP
\fBStrict vs best\-effort packetize\fR
strict aborts the record on the first malformed KLV packet; best\-effort scans forward, recovers what it can, and surfaces diagnostics with byte offsets. Use best\-effort for forensic recovery on suspect input.
.TP
\fBStructural validation\fR
klvtool's per\-tag checks (length, range, encoding) against the registered MISB Local Set spec. Failures appear as error\-severity diagnostics on the relevant packet.
.TP
\fBView modes (auto/pretty/raw)\fR
auto picks pretty when stdout is a TTY and the format is human\-oriented, raw otherwise. Pretty is for humans (color, multi\-line); raw is for piping (single\-line, no escapes).
.TP
\fBLikely metadata stream\fR
a PID whose stream type is one of the data/private types commonly used to carry KLV. `klvtool inspect` highlights these in pretty view; `klvtool diagnose` decodes them automatically.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
\fB0\fR
success
.TP
\fB1\fR
command\-specific failure (see the per\-command page)
.TP
\fB2\fR
invalid usage
.SH "ENVIRONMENT"
.TP
\fBNO_COLOR\fR
